# Do people care about fairness?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What happens in the ultimatum game? Why do people turn down free money?"
next: {"title":"Why tip a waiter you will never see again?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/92-why-tip-a-waiter-you-will-never-see.cast"}

## Your offer
sources:
    - id: guth1982
      title: An experimental analysis of ultimatum bargaining
      authors: W. Güth, R. Schmittberger, B. Schwarze
      year: 1982
      doi: 10.1016/0167-2681(82)90011-7
      finding: 'The first ultimatum game experiments, with economics graduate students at the University of Cologne: many proposers did not take nearly everything, and some offers were rejected.'
    - id: oosterbeek2004
      title: 'Cultural differences in ultimatum game experiments: evidence from a meta-analysis'
      authors: H. Oosterbeek, R. Sloof, G. van de Kuilen
      year: 2004
      finding: Across 37 papers, proposers offer on average about 40 % of the pie.
    - id: camerer2003
      title: 'Behavioral Game Theory: Experiments in Strategic Interaction'
      authors: C. F. Camerer
      year: 2003
      finding: Across many ultimatum studies, modal and median offers are 40–50 % of the pie; offers below about 20 % are rejected about half the time.

    icon you size 150 x 180 y 540 icon_look picture of person hidden true
    text you_l "You" #2f6b8f x 180 y 640 font_size 34 hidden true
    icon stranger size 150 x 820 y 540 icon_look picture of person hidden true
    text stranger_l "Stranger" #b5482e x 820 y 640 font_size 34 hidden true
    box cash "$100" x 500 y 560 width 200 height 90 icon ["money bag"] font_size 40 hidden true
    text rule_yes "Accept: split happens" #87a878 x 500 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    text rule_no "Reject: both get $0" #b5482e x 500 y 430 font_size 30 hidden true
    scale offerline x 150 y 200 width 700 font_size 30 label "Your offer ($)" min 0 max 100 value 1 ticks 10 markers [{"value":40,"label":"average ≈ 40","color":"#3d7a3d"}] hidden true
    text tb_l "textbook" #8f887c x 242 y 248 font_size 28 hidden true
    polygon band_fair cites ["oosterbeek2004","camerer2003"] points [[430,204],[500,204],[500,240],[430,240]] style.fill #87a878 style.opacity 0.55 style.stroke none hidden true
    text band_fair_l "most offers" #3d7a3d x 615 y 260 font_size 28 hidden true
    polygon band_low cites ["camerer2003"] points [[150,204],[290,204],[290,240],[150,240]] style.fill #c96567 style.opacity 0.5 style.stroke none hidden true
    text band_low_l "often rejected" #b5482e x 220 y 125 font_size 28 hidden true
    text guth "Güth, Schmittberger & Schwarze · Cologne 1982" cites ["guth1982"] x 500 y 450 font_size 28 hidden true
    text kof "Kindness or fear?" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 450 font_size 48 hidden true

    card title "Your offer"

Do people care about fairness, enough to turn down free money? A simple game puts a price on the answer.
    draw you you_l stranger stranger_l

B: Say I hand you  (@draw cash@)a hundred dollars, illustrative money, and you must offer part of it to a stranger.

If the (@draw rule_yes@) stranger accepts, you both keep your sh (@draw rule_no@)ares. If they refuse, you both get nothing. That's the ultimatum game.

B: Before any theory: how much would you offer?
    draw offerline

    ask "How many of the 100 dollars do you offer the stranger? Click on the line." on offerline judge false store o default "25" voice b
        right: You offered {o} dollars. The textbook offers one.

    erase rule_yes rule_no

Now the textbook answer. A stranger who  (@draw tb_l@)only wants money prefers one dollar to nothing, so you offer one dollar and keep ninety-nine.

B: Cold, but logical. Did anyone actually play it that way?
    highlight offerline_answer

Hardly anyone. Werne (@draw guth@)r Güth and two colleagues first ran the game with students in Cologne in 1982.

Across dozens of studies sinc (@draw band_fair band_fair_l@)e, offers average about  (@draw offerline_marker_1@)forty percent, and an even split is common.
    fade offerline_answer tb_l to 0.3

B: And the stingy offers? Belo (@draw band_low band_low_l@)w about twenty percent, they're turned down about half the time.

    erase guth tb_l

So real people refuse free money. And the generous offers raise a seco (@draw kof@)nd question: kindness, or fear of that refusal?

## In the stranger's seat
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation: U = x - alpha*(100 - 2*x)
    params:
      alpha: {value: 0, min: 0, max: 2, step: 0.1, label: envy weight α, color: '#b5482e'}
    x_range:
      - 0
      - 50
    y_range:
      - -100
      - 50
    x_label: Offer x ($)
    y_label: U of accepting
    marks:
      - {kind: roots, label: false}
    drag: false
sources:
    - id: fehrschmidt1999
      title: A theory of fairness, competition, and cooperation
      authors: E. Fehr, K. M. Schmidt
      year: 1999
      finding: 'Inequity aversion: utility is own payoff minus a weight on being behind (and a smaller one on being ahead); it predicts that responders reject offers below a threshold that rises with that weight.'

    box offer "You get $10 · they keep $90" x 500 y 520 width 520 height 90 font_size 34 hidden true
    text acc "Accept: you +$10" #2f6b8f x 500 y 400 font_size 32 hidden true
    text rej "Reject: they get $0" #b5482e x 500 y 350 font_size 32 hidden true
    text fs "Fehr & Schmidt 1999" cites ["fehrschmidt1999"] x 500 y 260 font_size 30 hidden true
    portrait fehr x 860 y 330 width 180 of "Ernst Fehr" cameo true hidden true
    text zone_rej "Reject" #b5482e at.data [8,-45] font_size 30 hidden true
    text zone_acc "Accept" #2f6b8f at.data [12,40] font_size 30 hidden true
    text selfish "rational ≠ selfish" #8a5fa8 at.data [33,-75] font_size 34 hidden true
    text thr "≈ 33: smallest accepted" #3d3833 at.data [40,-30] font_size 28 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after_math"}] then after_math hidden true
    scratch mb work [{"tex":"x - \\alpha(100 - 2x) \\ge 0"},{"tex":"x(1 + 2\\alpha) \\ge 100\\alpha"},{"tex":"x^* = \\frac{100\\alpha}{1 + 2\\alpha}"},"α = 1 → 33;  α huge → 50"] x 770 y 145 font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "In the stranger's seat"

To see why anyone refuses (@draw offer@), swap seats. Now you're the stranger.

B: Someone holding a hundred offers you ten and keeps ninety. Accept or reject?
    quiz "You're offered $10 of $100. Do you accept or reject?" judge false
        right: Either answer is common. Here is what each one buys.
        * Accept
        * Reject

Accep (@draw acc@)ting buys you ten dollars. Rejecting buys som (@draw rej@)ething too: the proposer walks away with nothing.

B: Spite, then. Or a sense of fairness, with a cost attached. Er (@draw fehr@)nst Fehr and Klaus Sch (@draw fs@)midt wrote it down in 1999.

    erase offer acc rej fs fehr

Your utility is your money, x, minus a penalty for being behind: an envy weight, alpha, times the gap, a hundred minus two x.
    draw axes eq curve_0

B: And alpha is how much falling behind stings. At alpha equals zero, that's the textbook stranger.
    highlight eq_param_alpha

With alpha at zero, the line (@draw zone_acc zone_rej@) never dips below zero. Any offer beats nothing, so you take it.

Raise alpha to one, and the line drops below zero for every offer under about  (@draw root_1 thr@)thirty-three. Those get rejected.
    animate params.alpha 1 duration 3 ghost true

Try it: slide alpha and watch where the line crosses zero, the smallest offer this stranger will take.
    explore

B: So turning down ten dollars isn't a mistake. It's a (@draw selfish@) choice, made by someone who wants more than money.

Rational never meant selfish, as Becker insisted. The ultimatum game just puts a price on the unselfish part.
    highlight selfish

    erase selfish

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: There's a one-line formula for that smallest offer.

@math

    erase mathq

    hide thr

Accept when your utility is at least zero.
    draw mb_box mb_line_1

Gather the x terms on one side.
    draw mb_line_2

So the smallest offer you take is a hundred alpha, over one plus two alpha.
    draw mb_line_3

At alpha equals one that's thirty-three; however large alpha gets, it never passes fifty.
    draw mb_line_4

    erase mb

@after_math

    erase mathq

    show thr

## Kindness or fear?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Ultimatum
      - Dictator
    values:
      - 40
      - 28
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 0
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 50
    y_label: Average share offered
    x_label: averages across many studies
    gap: 0.7
    label_size: 28
    bar_colors: each
    colors:
      - red
      - purple
    reference:
      value: 40
      label: with a veto
sources:
    - id: forsythe1994
      title: Fairness in simple bargaining experiments
      authors: R. Forsythe, J. L. Horowitz, N. E. Savin, M. Sefton
      year: 1994
      doi: 10.1006/game.1994.1021
      finding: 'Ran the dictator game beside the ultimatum game: without the veto, offers fall well below ultimatum offers, but many dictators still give something.'
    - id: oosterbeek2004
      title: 'Cultural differences in ultimatum game experiments: evidence from a meta-analysis'
      authors: H. Oosterbeek, R. Sloof, G. van de Kuilen
      year: 2004
      finding: Across 37 papers, ultimatum proposers offer on average about 40 % of the pie.
    - id: engel2011
      title: 'Dictator games: a meta study'
      authors: C. Engel
      year: 2011
      doi: 10.1007/s10683-011-9283-7
      finding: Across more than a hundred published dictator experiments, dictators give on average about 28 % of the pie; many give nothing, but most give something.
    - id: ultcast
      title: You have $100 to split with a stranger. How much would you offer?
      authors: drawcast library (hmelberg)
      year: 2026
      url: https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/casts/ultimatum-game.cast
      finding: 'An earlier drawcast on the ultimatum game: people offer what they expect to be accepted.'

    text noveto "no veto: take what you're given" #8a5fa8 x 660 y 615 font_size 30 hidden true
    text fors "Forsythe et al. 1994" cites ["forsythe1994"] x 660 y 615 font_size 30 hidden true
    text fear "fear of a veto ≈ 12" #b5482e x 790 y 460 font_size 32 hidden true
    text fair "no veto:\nfairness?" #8a5fa8 x 880 y 330 font_size 32 hidden true
    link credit href https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/casts/ultimatum-game.cast title "After: “You have $100 to split” · drawcast.app" form text cites ["ultcast"] x 620 y 615 hidden true
    text resp "responder: pays to punish" #b5482e x 640 y 615 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Kindness or fear?"

So responders pay to punish low offers. That leaves the proposers: are they being fair, or just careful?
    draw axes

B: Take away the s (@draw noveto@)tranger's veto. The proposer decides, and the other side simply gets what they're given.

    erase noveto

That's the dictator game. In 1994 Robert For (@draw fors@)sythe and colleagues ran it side by side with the ultimatum game.

Across many ul (@draw bar_1@)timatum studies, the average offer is about forty percent.

    ask "Drag the dictator bar to the share you think dictators give." on bar_2 store g default "10" voice b
        intro: Your guess: with no veto at all, what share do dictators hand over on average?
        right: About twenty-eight percent: close to your {g}%.
        wrong: You said {g}%: it's about twenty-eight.

About twenty-eight percent, averaged over more than a hundred experiments. Many give nothing, but most give something.
    highlight bar_2

    erase fors

Roughly, m (@draw reference_1@)uch of that twelve-point dro (@draw fear@)p is fear of rejection. That part is strategy, not virtue.

B: And the twenty-eight that's left is what people give w (@draw fair@)hen nobody can punish them. That's closer to fairness.

B: An earlier draw (@draw credit@)cast, "You have $100 to split with a stranger", puts it in a line: we offer what we expect to be accepted. True, but only half the story.

    erase credit

The cleane (@fade bar_1 reference_1 fear to 0.3@)r fairness signal is the responder: a (@draw resp@) refusal costs real money and buys nothing but the point.

## Raise the stakes
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Fair offers, fair score.
    good: An offer most responders would accept.
    poor: A responder might reject that score, but take another look.
    none: 'Even a stingy offer beats nothing: try again.'
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Rs 20 · 2 h pay
      - Rs 200 · 2 days
      - Rs 2,000 · 6 wks
      - Rs 20,000 · 1 yr
    values:
      - 36
      - 43
      - 28
      - 4
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 0
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 50
    y_label: Offers rejected
    x_label: 'India 2011: stake to split'
    gap: 0.6
    label_size: 28
    bar_colors: same
sources:
    - id: cameron1999
      title: 'Raising the stakes in the ultimatum game: experimental evidence from Indonesia'
      authors: L. A. Cameron
      year: 1999
      doi: 10.1111/j.1465-7295.1999.tb01415.x
      finding: 'Stakes up to three times the average participant''s monthly expenditure: responders become more willing to accept a given percentage offer at higher stakes, but proposer behaviour is largely invariant; results do not approach the selfish prediction.'
    - id: andersen2011
      title: Stakes matter in ultimatum games
      authors: S. Andersen, S. Ertaç, U. Gneezy, M. Hoffman, J. A. List
      year: 2011
      doi: 10.1257/aer.101.7.3427
      finding: Eight villages in Meghalaya, India; stakes of Rs 20, 200, 2,000 and 20,000 (1.6 to 1,600 hours of work; Rs 20,000 a little over a year's income). Instructions elicited low offers (median 20 % of the pie). Rejection rates 36 %, 43 %, 28 % and 4 % (1 of 24) by stake.

    scratch cam cites ["cameron1999"] work ["Indonesia 1999","stakes ≈ 3 months' spending","offers: still fair","rejections: fewer, not gone"] x 560 y 430 font_size 34 hidden true
    text and "Andersen, List et al. 2011" cites ["andersen2011"] x 690 y 620 font_size 28 hidden true
    text nudge "offers nudged low by design" #8f887c x 690 y 580 font_size 26 hidden true
    text one24 "1 of 24" #b5482e x 850 y 265 font_size 30 hidden true
    text st1 "Everyday stakes: consensus" #3d7a3d x 760 y 560 font_size 34 hidden true
    text st2 "Huge stakes: contested" #b5482e x 760 y 505 font_size 34 hidden true
    text cult "Every culture alike? Its own lecture" #8f887c x 760 y 450 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Raise the stakes"

Refusing ten dollars is cheap. Would anyone refuse a month's pay, or a year's?

B: Lisa Came (@draw cam_box cam_line_1 cam_line_2@)ron raised the stakes in Indonesia in 1999, up to about three months' spending.

Proposers ba (@draw cam_line_3@)rely changed: offers stayed fair. Responders grew  (@draw cam_line_4@)somewhat more willing to accept, but rejections didn't vanish.

B: Hold on. If offers stay fair, we never learn how people answer a really stingy offer when the money is big.
    highlight cam_line_3

    erase cam

In 2011 Steffen Andersen (@draw axes and@), John List and colleagues closed that gap in villages in northeast India, with instru (@draw nudge@)ctions that nudged proposers toward low offers.

B: The stakes ran from about two hours' pay to about a year's income.  (@draw bar_1@)At the three lower stakes, th (@draw bar_2@)irty-six, forty-three and tw (@draw bar_3@)enty-eight percent of offers were rejected. Your guess for the top?

    ask "Drag the last bar to the share of offers you think were rejected at a year's income." on bar_4 store g default "25" voice b
        right: About four percent: close to your {g}%.
        wrong: You said {g}%. The truth is far lower: about four percent, one in twenty-four.

About four percent: one rej (@draw one24@)ection out of twenty-four.

So fairness behaves like a preference with a price. At a year's income, almost nobody pays it.
    highlight bar_4

    erase and nudge

B: How sure are we? That people pay to punish unfairness at everyday stake (@draw st1@)s is replicated in labs around the world: consensus. That it fades at huge stakes rests mai (@draw st2@)nly on one large field study: contested.

And whether every culture  (@draw cult@)plays the game alike is a question with a lecture of its own.

    quiz "A charity lets donors give anonymously or have their name put on a plaque. Which motive does the plaque add?"
        right: Wanting to look generous: kindness works just as well in secret, so the plaque adds being seen.
        wrong: Kindness works in secret too, and nobody can reject a gift: here, wanting to look generous.
        + Wanting to look generous
        * Fear of rejection
        * Kindness

    quiz "A responder's envy weight α is 0.5. She is offered 30 of 100, so U = 30 − 0.5 × (100 − 60). Does she accept?" voice b
        right: Accept: 30 − 0.5 × 40 = 10, still above zero.
        wrong: Work it out: 30 − 0.5 × 40 is 10, above zero: she accepts.
        + Accept
        * Reject

    quiz "A game-show contestant must offer a rival part of a $1 million prize. If the rival vetoes, both get nothing. She offers $100,000. What does the high-stakes evidence suggest?"
        right: Most likely accepted: a stingy share of a fortune is still a fortune, and refusing it is too costly for most.
        wrong: When refusing costs a year's income or more, almost nobody pays to punish: here, most likely accepted.
        + Most likely accepted
        * Most likely rejected
        * Accepted only by a purely selfish rival

So do people care about fairness? Yes, enough to pay real money for it, though not at any price. Economics keeps self-interest, and adds fairness to the list of things people want.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4

